# ============================================================
# HHI Netherlands — legacy URL redirects
#
# The old site was PHP and is indexed at .php URLs. The migrated
# site serves clean routes (/sponsors), so without these rules
# every inbound link from search results, socials and press
# 404s once the legacy host is switched off.
#
# WRITTEN 14 Aug 2026, NOT YET VERIFIED ON THE REAL HOST.
# Cloud86 runs LiteSpeed, which reads Apache .htaccess, but
# whether it applies these as expected on their configuration is
# untestable until the account exists. Verify on cutover day:
#   curl -sI https://hhi-netherlands.com/sponsors.php
#   -> expect 301 with Location: /sponsors
#
# The legacy URLs below were taken from the live site's own nav
# on 14 Aug 2026, not guessed. Note it is .php that matters:
# the .html variants answer 200 but serve a 43-byte stub, and
# so does any nonexistent name, so .html was never a real URL.
# ============================================================

RewriteEngine On

# ============================================================
# SECURITY AND TRANSPORT
#
# Added 21 Aug 2026. Everything above this point is a redirect
# map; none of it protects anything. These rules do.
#
# ⚠️ SAME CAVEAT AS THE REDIRECTS: unverified on the real host.
# The HTTPS rule is the one entry here that can take the site
# down rather than merely not working — if Cloud86 terminates
# TLS at a proxy and does not set X-Forwarded-Proto, a naive
# rule loops forever. The two-condition form below is written
# to survive that, but verify on cutover day before trusting:
#   curl -sI http://hhi-netherlands.com/     -> 301 to https
#   curl -sI https://www.hhi-netherlands.com/ -> 301 to bare
#   curl -sI https://hhi-netherlands.com/     -> 200, no loop
# If the first check loops, comment the HTTPS block out and use
# Cloud86's panel-level Force HTTPS instead.
# ============================================================

# --- Force HTTPS ------------------------------------------------
# The contact form POSTs names, emails and messages; over plain
# http that is readable in transit. The canonical tags in the
# built HTML already claim https, so this makes the server agree.
#
# Two conditions, not one. %{HTTPS} is what Apache sets when it
# terminated TLS itself; X-Forwarded-Proto is what a proxy in
# front sets when IT did. Checking only the first redirects an
# already-secure proxied request back to itself, forever.
RewriteCond %{HTTPS} !=on
RewriteCond %{HTTP:X-Forwarded-Proto} !https
RewriteRule ^ https://%{HTTP_HOST}%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]

# --- Canonical host ---------------------------------------------
# Both hostnames answer, so every page exists at two URLs and
# search engines split the ranking between them. The bare domain
# wins because SITE_URL in src/lib/config.ts is already
# 'https://hhi-netherlands.com' and every canonical/og:url tag is
# built from it — redirecting the other way would contradict the
# markup. Change both together or neither.
#
# %1 is the capture from the RewriteCond, i.e. the host minus www.
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^www\.(.+)$ [NC]
RewriteRule ^ https://%1%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]

# --- Protect the SMTP credentials -------------------------------
# api/contact.secret.php holds a live mailbox password. It is
# gitignored and excluded from the FTP mirror, so it cannot leak
# through the repo or be erased by a deploy — but that is all
# about getting it TO the server, and says nothing about who can
# read it once there.
#
# The failure this guards is narrow and real: if PHP ever stops
# handling .php on this host — a bad .htaccess edit, a panel
# change, a PHP version switch — the server falls back to serving
# the file as plain text, and the password is public at a URL a
# scanner will guess. Denying it outright costs nothing, because
# contact.php reads it from disk and never over HTTP.
#
# One FilesMatch rather than a name plus a pattern: the pattern
# already covers contact.secret.php, and anything else that ever
# takes a .secret.php name is covered the day it is created.
#
# Both syntaxes on purpose. "Require" is Apache 2.4 and is what
# LiteSpeed should honour; "Order/Deny" is the 2.2 spelling, kept
# because a host silently running in 2.2-compat mode would treat
# Require as unknown and 500 the whole site. Each is wrapped in
# its own IfModule so only the one the server understands is ever
# parsed, and an unrecognised directive can never take the site
# down to protect one file.
<FilesMatch "\.secret\.php$">
  <IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
    Require all denied
  </IfModule>
  <IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
    Order allow,deny
    Deny from all
  </IfModule>
</FilesMatch>

# --- No directory listings --------------------------------------
# /download/, /img/ and the eleven gallery division folders hold
# no index file. With Indexes on — which is the default on plenty
# of shared hosts — each one is browsable, publishing the whole
# file inventory including ~950 gallery photos nobody linked.
# The files are public; the exhaustive list of them need not be.
#
# ⚠️ -MultiViews is deliberate and must stay, even though it may
# look redundant next to the extensionless rewrite below. The two
# are not doing the same job:
#
# MultiViews is mod_negotiation's content guessing — asked for
# /media it hunts for a file whose name starts "media" and serves
# whatever it likes the look of. That sounds like exactly what we
# want, but it picks by its own negotiation rules, not ours: with
# both media.html and a hypothetical media.php present it may
# choose either, and it answers 200 for near-misses that should be
# a clean 404. Leaving it ON would mask whether the explicit rule
# below actually works, so a later edit could break the rule and
# nothing would show it until MultiViews was also switched off.
#
# Turning it off makes the rewrite the single, readable reason any
# extensionless URL resolves. Both options are named on one line
# because a bare `Options -Indexes` REPLACES the inherited set
# rather than subtracting from it on some configurations, which is
# how MultiViews silently flipped state here in the first place.
Options -Indexes -MultiViews

# --- Extensionless page URLs ------------------------------------
# THE FIX for the 404-on-refresh reported 21 Aug 2026, verified
# against the live host: curl -sI https://hhi-netherlands.com/media
# returned 404 while clicking through to the same page worked.
#
# Why it happens: adapter-static is called with no options in
# vite.config.ts, so the build writes FLAT files — build/media.html,
# not build/media/index.html. Every canonical tag, every sitemap
# <loc> and every internal href is the extensionless /media, so
# in-site navigation never asks the server anything (SvelteKit's
# client router handles it in JS) but a refresh, a bookmark, a
# pasted link or a crawler asks Apache for a file literally named
# "media" — which does not exist.
#
# An INTERNAL rewrite, not a redirect. /media must keep answering
# 200 at /media: a 301 to /media.html would contradict the
# canonical tag, the og:url and all ten sitemap entries, and would
# publish the .html form as the real URL — the exact shape the
# legacy-redirect block below exists to retire.
#
# Placed BEFORE the legacy .php/.html redirect map on purpose. The
# guards below mean this rule only fires for a request that has a
# real .html twin, and no legacy URL does, so the two cannot fight
# — but keeping the silent internal rewrite ahead of the noisy
# 301s also keeps the reading order "resolve real pages, then
# forward dead ones".
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d
RewriteCond %{DOCUMENT_ROOT}/$1.html -f
RewriteRule ^(.+?)/?$ /$1.html [L]

# --- Response headers -------------------------------------------
# Wrapped in IfModule so the file still parses if mod_headers is
# unavailable: a missing header degrades to today's behaviour,
# while an unrecognised directive would 500 the entire site.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  # Stop browsers guessing a MIME type different from the one
  # sent. The classic exploit is a user-uploaded file sniffed as
  # HTML and executed in-origin.
  Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"

  # Send the full URL to same-origin requests, bare origin to
  # cross-origin ones, nothing when leaving https for http.
  Header always set Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"

  # The site has no reason to be framed. Blocks the clickjacking
  # dressing-up of /contact or /registration inside another page.
  Header always set X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"

  # None of these features are used; deny them rather than leave
  # the defaults to whatever a future browser decides.
  Header always set Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), interest-cohort=()"
</IfModule>


# --- Main pages -------------------------------------------------
# One line per legacy page. R=301 (permanent) so search engines
# transfer ranking rather than treating these as temporary.
RewriteRule ^index\.php$           /               [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^events\.php$          /events         [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^registration\.php$    /registration   [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^organisation\.php$    /organisation   [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^sponsors\.php$        /sponsors       [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^contact\.php$         /contact        [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^regulations\.php$     /regulations    [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^results\.php$         /results        [R=301,L]

# The legacy site split media across two pages; the migrated site
# has one /media route covering both photos and videos.
RewriteRule ^photos\.php$          /media          [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^videos\.php$          /media          [R=301,L]

# Ticketing was its own page that only embedded the ticket shop.
# It pointed at the OLD shop.compoticketing.eu; the current shop
# is shop.celebratix.io, which is off-site, so this cannot go to
# a local route. Sent to /events, which carries the tickets CTA.
RewriteRule ^tickets\.php$         /events         [R=301,L]

# --- Photo archive ----------------------------------------------
# 71 gallery pages (hhi2010.php … hhinl2026m.php) holding ~8,163
# photos. The migrated site has no equivalent route, so these
# collapse to /media rather than 404. If the archive is ever
# republished, give it real routes and replace this rule.
RewriteRule ^hhi(nl)?[0-9]{4}[a-z]?\.php$  /media  [R=301,L]

# --- Privacy policy ---------------------------------------------
# Held unredirected until 15 Aug 2026, because sending a legal
# document people go looking for specifically to the home page is
# worse than a 404. It now has a real route carrying the same
# Dutch text, so the rule this file asked for is here.
RewriteRule ^privacy-policy\.php$  /privacy  [R=301,L]

# --- Trailing .html ---------------------------------------------
# The .html URLs never served real content, but they were linked
# often enough over the years to be worth catching. Handled with
# one rule rather than nine, since the mapping is identical.
RewriteRule ^(events|registration|organisation|sponsors|contact|regulations|results)\.html$  /$1  [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^privacy-policy\.html$  /privacy  [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^(index|home)\.html$   /               [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^(photos|videos|media)\.html$  /media  [R=301,L]
